# network.env — per-site network settings for provision-card.sh.
#
# THIS FILE IS AN EXAMPLE. Copy it outside the repository:
#
#     cp scripts/network.env.example ~/.rpi0-secrets/network.env
#     $EDITOR ~/.rpi0-secrets/network.env
#
# provision-card.sh reads $SECRETS_DIR/network.env (default
# ~/.rpi0-secrets/network.env) and, if a static address is configured
# there, writes it onto the card as a networkd drop-in at
# /etc/systemd/network/20-wlan0.network.d/10-static.conf.
#
# Everything here is OPTIONAL. With no network.env at all, the card keeps
# the image's own default, which is DHCP, and the board is reachable on any
# LAN it can associate with. That fallback is deliberate: the safe path is
# what you get when something goes wrong.
#
# Nothing in this file is a secret — an IP address is public information
# the moment the board sends a packet. It lives outside the repo for the
# other reason: it describes the SITE, not the product, and an image with a
# site's address compiled into it only works at that site.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY BOTHER: THE MEASUREMENT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# On the Zero W, going static removed about 4 seconds from time-to-
# reachable, and it did NOT move the number systemd-analyze prints. Both
# halves of that sentence matter, and notes/22 is about why.
#
# The 4 s was never the router being slow. Over five instrumented boots the
# FIRST DHCP exchange failed every single time — 5/5, so not a race, a race
# sometimes wins. networkd then waits out RFC 2131's retransmission backoff,
# measured at 4 +/- 1 s of uniform jitter, and the retry succeeds in about
# 25 ms. So the cost was one lost packet plus a standards-mandated pause.
#
# A static address does not make that conversation faster. It deletes the
# conversation. Claiming an address is a local netlink call to the kernel,
# not a request to anybody: there is no packet that can be lost and no timer
# to roll dice against. Which is why the saving is deterministic rather than
# the quantised 4-or-9 seconds DHCP was giving us.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BEFORE YOU USE ONE: RESERVE IT ON THE ROUTER
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A LAN has no authority that grants addresses. The host simply claims one
# and answers ARP for it; DHCP is a bookkeeping convention that hosts opt
# into, not an enforcement mechanism. So a static address the DHCP server
# does not know about is an address it will happily hand to somebody else
# once the board's old lease ages out — and then two hosts answer the same
# ARP query, with no referee. The symptom is intermittent and asymmetric and
# looks exactly like flaky hardware.
#
# Reserve the address for the board's MAC on the router. On ASUS that is
# LAN -> DHCP Server -> "Manually Assigned IP around the DHCP list". The
# reservation does not speed anything up — the board is not asking any more
# — it just stops the server giving the address away.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SETTINGS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The address, WITH a prefix length. The prefix is not decoration: it is how
# the kernel decides which addresses are on the local wire (reachable
# directly by ARP) and which have to go via the gateway.
STATIC_ADDR=192.168.50.34/24

# Where anything outside that prefix is sent. Must be inside the address's
# subnet — provision-card.sh refuses otherwise, because a gateway outside it
# produces a next-hop ARP can never resolve, and therefore a board that
# boots perfectly and is silently unreachable.
#
# Use the literal word "none" for a deliberately LAN-only board. There is no
# way to simply omit this: forgetting a gateway and choosing not to have one
# produce the same file but very different intentions, and the script will
# not guess which you meant.
STATIC_GW=192.168.50.1

# Resolver for the networkd drop-in's DNS= line. Defaults to STATIC_GW.
#
# NOTE this is NOT what gives the board a working resolver. DNS= in a
# .network file is consumed by systemd-resolved, and this image is built
# without it (see recipes-core/systemd/systemd_%.bbappend). The line is
# written for correctness if resolved ever comes back; today it is inert.
# What actually resolves names is RESOLVERS below, which lands in
# /etc/resolv.conf.
#STATIC_DNS=192.168.50.1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A BOARD THAT MOVES: DHCP, AND THE TWO THINGS THAT MUST STILL WORK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything above assumes the board lives on one known LAN. Take it to a
# customer site, or tether it to a phone hotspot, and a static address is
# exactly wrong: the subnet belongs to somebody else and is not knowable in
# advance. Provision that card with
#
#     STATIC_ADDR=none ./scripts/provision-card.sh /dev/sdX
#
# which overrides the STATIC_ADDR set above for that one run and leaves the
# image's own DHCP default in place. Then find the board with
# ./scripts/find-board.sh, which sweeps whatever subnet the laptop is on and
# matches the board by MAC.
#
# The address is then unknown, but two things still have to be right, and
# neither can be learned from a network we have never seen. Both are written
# unconditionally by provision-card.sh, and both default to public anycast
# addresses for exactly that reason.

# What goes in /etc/resolv.conf, space-separated, in preference order.
#
# Public on purpose. A router-local resolver (192.168.50.1) is correct on
# precisely one network and answers nothing anywhere else — and since the
# only name this board ever looks up is an NTP server, a dead resolver
# doesn't show up as a DNS error, it shows up as a clock that never gets set.
#RESOLVERS="1.1.1.1 8.8.8.8"

# What goes in /etc/systemd/timesyncd.conf.d/10-ntp.conf.
#
# IP addresses, not hostnames, and that is the point. timesyncd's
# compiled-in fallback on this image is time1-4.google.com — verified with
# strings(1) against the binary in the rootfs — so out of the box the clock
# depends on DNS, which depends on RESOLVERS above being right. Naming the
# servers by address takes the resolver out of the path entirely.
#
# This board has no RTC, so with no time source it boots to whatever
# /var/lib/systemd/timesync/clock last recorded. The serial logger names its
# files from the clock, so that is the difference between a day of logs you
# can correlate with events and a directory full of session_ttyACM0_2025-05-29.log.
#
# Defaults are Google and Cloudflare NTP anycast.
#NTP_SERVERS="216.239.35.0 216.239.35.4 162.159.200.1 162.159.200.123"

# The board's wlan0 MAC, used by ./scripts/find-board.sh to pick it out of a
# subnet sweep. Not required — the script falls back to matching Raspberry Pi
# OUI prefixes — but the fallback matches EVERY Pi on the network, which on
# this bench means it finds the Pi 5 too. Record it once:
#
#     on the board:  ip link show wlan0
#
# It is the one property of the board that does not change when it moves.
#BOARD_MAC=b8:27:eb:xx:xx:xx

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE HYBRID — NOW THE DEFAULT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# When STATIC_ADDR is set, provision-card.sh writes a drop-in that claims the
# address AND keeps a DHCP client running alongside it:
#
#     [Network]
#     Address=192.168.50.34/24
#     DHCP=ipv4
#
#     [Route]
#     Gateway=192.168.50.1
#     Metric=2048
#
# On this LAN the board is reachable the instant it has carrier, because
# claiming an address is a netlink call to the local kernel — no packet, no
# timer, nothing to lose. On any other network the static address is inert
# and DHCP is what gets an address. One card works at both sites with no
# re-provisioning in between.
#
# Metric=2048 is the load-bearing line and it is backwards from what you would
# naturally write. networkd gives DHCP-learned default routes metric 1024, and
# LOWER wins. At its default metric the static route would be preferred
# everywhere — including on a network where this gateway does not exist, where
# it would be installed, preferred, and then blackhole every packet leaving
# the LAN. 2048 lets DHCP win wherever a DHCP server exists.
#
# This became the default on 2026-08-25, and the reason is better than the
# original one. The argument for pure static had been that it saves ~4 s
# (notes/23: the first DHCP exchange failed on 5 boots out of 5, costing
# RFC 2131's backoff). Then this LAN spent an evening leaving the board with
# carrier at 18 s and no address until 336 s — reproducible to within two
# seconds across boots, cause still unknown (notes/25). A configuration that
# is merely FAST when the network cooperates is worth much less than one that
# is reachable when it does not. The hybrid is both, and its only cost is a
# DHCP client running in the background that nothing waits for.
#
# Set this to "no" for the old pure-static behaviour (DHCP=no). Do that only
# for a board that will never leave this LAN: a card provisioned that way and
# carried elsewhere boots perfectly and is unreachable.
#STATIC_DHCP_FALLBACK=no

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A BOARD THAT MOVES: DHCP, AND THE TWO THINGS THAT MUST STILL WORK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything above assumes the board lives on one known LAN. Take it to a
# customer site, or tether it to a phone hotspot, and a static address is
# exactly wrong: the subnet belongs to somebody else and is not knowable in
# advance. Provision that card with
#
#     STATIC_ADDR=none ./scripts/provision-card.sh /dev/sdX
#
# which overrides the STATIC_ADDR set above for that one run and leaves the
# image's own DHCP default in place. Then find the board with
# ./scripts/find-board.sh, which sweeps whatever subnet the laptop is on and
# matches the board by MAC.
#
# The address is then unknown, but two things still have to be right, and
# neither can be learned from a network we have never seen. Both are written
# unconditionally by provision-card.sh, and both default to public anycast
# addresses for exactly that reason.

# What goes in /etc/resolv.conf, space-separated, in preference order.
#
# Public on purpose. A router-local resolver (192.168.50.1) is correct on
# precisely one network and answers nothing anywhere else — and since the
# only name this board ever looks up is an NTP server, a dead resolver
# doesn't show up as a DNS error, it shows up as a clock that never gets set.
#RESOLVERS="1.1.1.1 8.8.8.8"

# What goes in /etc/systemd/timesyncd.conf.d/10-ntp.conf.
#
# IP addresses, not hostnames, and that is the point. timesyncd's
# compiled-in fallback on this image is time1-4.google.com — verified with
# strings(1) against the binary in the rootfs — so out of the box the clock
# depends on DNS, which depends on RESOLVERS above being right. Naming the
# servers by address takes the resolver out of the path entirely.
#
# This board has no RTC, so with no time source it boots to whatever
# /var/lib/systemd/timesync/clock last recorded. The serial logger names its
# files from the clock, so that is the difference between a day of logs you
# can correlate with events and a directory full of session_ttyACM0_2025-05-29.log.
#
# Defaults are Google and Cloudflare NTP anycast.
#NTP_SERVERS="216.239.35.0 216.239.35.4 162.159.200.1 162.159.200.123"

# The board's wlan0 MAC, used by ./scripts/find-board.sh to pick it out of a
# subnet sweep. Not required — the script falls back to matching Raspberry Pi
# OUI prefixes — but the fallback matches EVERY Pi on the network, which on
# this bench means it finds the Pi 5 too. Record it once:
#
#     on the board:  ip link show wlan0
#
# It is the one property of the board that does not change when it moves.
#BOARD_MAC=b8:27:eb:xx:xx:xx

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE HYBRID, IF THIS BOARD EVER MOVES BETWEEN NETWORKS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# provision-card.sh writes DHCP=no, i.e. pure static, because that is the
# honest configuration for a device living on one known LAN. If the board
# ever needs to survive being plugged into an unknown network, networkd will
# do both at once — hand-edit the drop-in on the card to:
#
#     [Network]
#     Address=192.168.50.34/24
#     DHCP=ipv4
#
#     [Route]
#     Gateway=192.168.50.1
#     Metric=2048
#
# The static address is configured immediately at carrier, so time-to-
# reachable stays where it is; DHCP runs alongside in the background and it
# no longer matters whether it takes 4 s or fails outright.
#
# The Metric=2048 is the non-obvious part, and it is backwards from what you
# would naturally write. networkd gives DHCP-learned default routes metric
# 1024, and LOWER wins. Leaving the static route at its default metric would
# make it preferred everywhere — including on a network where 192.168.50.1
# does not exist, where it would be installed, preferred, and then blackhole
# every packet leaving the LAN. Pushing it to 2048 makes DHCP win wherever a
# DHCP server exists and leaves the static gateway as the fallback for when
# none does.
